{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "noise": "L"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 135, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Restoring the direct input-output residual path when it is absent yields far larger gains than replacing an existing conv or pool on edge 3."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "With no node1->output edge, the cell has few paths to the output, so adding the direct skip matters more (experiments 17, 19, 20)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Filling an empty edge 3 with a 1x1 conv gives big gains, while replacing an existing skip or pool on edge 3 hurts."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Removing the direct path when node 1 also lacks an output edge starves the output node, giving drops as large as -56 points."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "A skip on edge 5 supplies the missing residual path when edge 3 is absent, so it helps strongly there but is neutral or harmful otherwise."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 5 is missing, edge 4 is one of the few routes into the output, so adding a conv there helps consistently."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "With a parameter-free edge 0, replacing the edge-4 conv with pooling removes much of the learned capacity on that path (experiment 87: about -20 points)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If edge 1 is also absent, removing edge 0 disconnects node 1 and collapses most of the cell's computation."}